From 757f8000e0a61a69fc3bfcbd4eb3d2ea57c0168a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 17:28:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: add a diagnostic for the settings-file read itself

Real 486DX2-50 data from patch 0019 (320x240 resolution request) came
back showing "Screen dimensions for fullscreen mode: 640x480" -- the
change never visibly took effect. Two increasingly specific theories
were checked and ruled out with real evidence, not assumption:

1. Settings-transfer gap (the ini files never reached the rig): ruled
   out -- vcctrl fetched settings/width.ini and height.ini directly off
   the real machine and their sha256 matched this repo's own
   build/stage/settings/*.ini exactly. The files genuinely say
   "320"/"240" on the real hardware.
2. CWD mismatch at launch (SettingsManager resolves "settings/
   width.ini" relative to the process's CWD, not the exe's own
   location -- confirmed by reading SettingsManager.cpp directly):
   ruled out -- vcctrl confirmed their launch procedure does `CD
   \DOSSAGE` before running DOSSAGE.EXE every round, landing CWD
   exactly where settings/ lives.

Read minorGems' File.h/PathDOS.cpp looking for a third mechanism (text-
mode CRLF-translation quirks, forward-slash delimiter handling) but
found nothing that should affect this specific 3-byte, no-newline file
on DJGPP (whose DOS Path implementation deliberately mirrors the Linux
one, per PathDOS.cpp's own comment, precisely because DJGPP's libc
handles '/' and POSIX-style paths the same way on real DOS).

Rather than keep guessing at a fourth mechanism, this instruments the
actual read directly: prints `SettingsManager::getIntSetting`'s
found-flag AND returned value for both "width" and "height" to stdout,
right where they're read (game.cpp, before the override-defaults
check). No new log file -- reuses the STDOUT.TXT redirect every real-
hardware round already captures. This pins down definitively whether
the divergence is at the File/Path layer (found=false despite the file
existing) or a parsing layer (found=true but the wrong value) --
either answer is useful, and guessing further without it risks chasing
a mechanism that isn't the real one, same lesson this whole
investigation has learned repeatedly.

Verified under DOSBox-X (redirected stdout this time, not just a
screenshot): `width found=1 value=320, height found=1 value=240` --
correct as always on this harness, confirms the diagnostic itself
works and prints exactly the expected format. Real-hardware data is
the actual test.
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index a51f471..61d6154 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -730,13 +730,32 @@ int mainFunction( int inArgCount, char **inArgs ) {
     char heightFound = false;
     int readHeight = SettingsManager::getIntSetting( "height",
                                                     &heightFound );
-    
+
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC: real-hardware data (486DX2-50, 2026-08-27)
+    // showed "Screen dimensions for fullscreen mode: 640x480" printed
+    // even after independently confirming (sha256, not TYPE+OCR) that
+    // settings/width.ini and height.ini genuinely say "320"/"240" on
+    // that exact machine, and that CWD at launch is correctly
+    // C:\DOSSAGE (settings/ resolves correctly relative to it) -- ruling
+    // out both a transfer gap and a CWD mismatch. This exposes exactly
+    // where the read diverges from the file content: whether
+    // SettingsManager::getIntSetting reports found=false at all (a
+    // File/Path-layer problem below this line) or found=true with the
+    // wrong value (a parsing problem). No new log file needed -- reuses
+    // the STDOUT.TXT redirect every real-hardware round already
+    // captures.
+    printf( "DOS-PORT settings diagnostic: width found=%d value=%d, "
+            "height found=%d value=%d\n",
+            (int)widthFound, readWidth, (int)heightFound, readHeight );
+#endif
+
     if( widthFound && heightFound ) {
         // override hard-coded defaults
         screenWidth = readWidth;
         screenHeight = readHeight;
         }
-    
+
     printf( "Screen dimensions for fullscreen mode:  %dx%d\n",
             screenWidth, screenHeight );
     
-- 
2.47.3

